{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfs1N5xJWNycS79WPZDQG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The slow `dedupe_rows` was the cause, and it's fixed. The ticket's 30k-row run reproduces it, and the fixed code passes the full suite.\n\n**Root cause:** `reporting/dedupe.py` kept the seen event IDs in a list (`seen_ids = []`). Every `in` check scanned the whole list, so de-duplication took time proportional to the square of the row count. The refactor did this to preserve first-occurrence order, but order is already kept by the separate `out` list.\n\n**Fix:** `seen_ids` is now a `set`. Lookups are constant-time, and the output is identical (same rows, same order).\n\n**Regression test:** `tests/test_report.py::test_dedupe_scales_linearly` de-duplicates 75k rows, half of them repeats. It checks the output order and that the call finishes in under 2 seconds.\n- With the old code it failed after 17.5 s.\n- With the fix, the whole suite passes (2 tests in 0.10 s).\n- The 2-second limit is wall-clock time, so it could be flaky on a very slow CI machine.\n\nMy first check of the test against the old code was wrong, because a stale result made it look like a pass. I re-ran it with the old file restored directly, and that run is the failure above.\n\nNothing is committed. `git status` also shows the tracked `__pycache__/*.pyc` files as modified; those are test-run byproducts, so you'll probably want to leave them out of a commit."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10255,"cache_read_input_tokens":47568,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10255},"output_tokens":479,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b882ea9a-451e-414d-80a1-8bd477e92ccb","uuid":